# Tidal Energy Datasets

This section provides tidal current datasets for five U.S. coastal sites with some of the highest tidal energy potential in the country. The datasets are produced using the Finite Volume Community Ocean Model (FVCOM) [@fvcom] and are designed to support tidal energy resource assessment, from initial site screening through turbine selection and project planning.

## Tidal Current Energy

Tidal currents in constricted coastal passages (inlets, straits, and river mouths) can reach sustained speeds of 2–4 m/s. A current energy converter (CEC) extracts this kinetic energy by intercepting the flow; the available power per unit rotor area is $P = \frac{1}{2}\rho U^3$, so sites with peak speeds of 2–3 m/s yield 4,100–13,800 W m⁻².

Tidal currents are astronomically forced and deterministic: speed and direction at any location can be predicted years in advance from a small set of harmonic constituents. This allows resource characterization to be expressed in terms of statistical quantities (mean power density, exceedance probability, spring-neap variability) that map directly onto project feasibility criteria and IEC assessment standards.

## Resource Characterization Workflow

Tidal energy resource assessment proceeds through three stages, each requiring a different level of the hindcast data.

### Stage 1: Site Screening

The [Marine Energy Atlas][marine-energy-atlas] displays mean current speed and power density across all five hindcast domains. Grid cells are color-coded by magnitude; the point-query tool returns summary statistics and supports CSV export. Sites where mean current speed exceeds ~1 m/s at hub depth are candidates for further assessment.

<!-- TODO: insert atlas walkthrough gif -->
!!! info "Atlas walkthrough coming soon"
    A short animated walkthrough of the Marine Energy Atlas, selecting a tidal layer, zooming to a region, and querying a grid cell, will appear here.

### Stage 2: Feasibility Assessment

Site-level feasibility analysis under [IEC TS 62600-201](high_resolution_hindcast/index.md) Stage 1 requires the following summary statistics:

- **Mean current speed** - time-averaged speed at each sigma layer; basis for annual energy estimates
- **95th-percentile current speed** - design-load threshold for structural analysis
- **Mean power density** - time-averaged kinetic energy flux per unit rotor area (W m⁻²)
- **Velocity exceedance curve** - cumulative distribution of current speed; used to estimate capacity factor

These statistics are available via the Atlas point-query tool or the [us-marine-energy-resource Python library][python-library]:

```python
import us_marine_energy_resource.tidal_hindcast as tidal

# Fetch the hindcast time series for the nearest grid point to a coordinate.
# Data is downloaded from S3 and cached locally on first call.
df = tidal.get_data_at_point(lat=60.73, lon=-151.43)

# Speed columns are named vap_sea_water_speed_layer_{0..9} (m/s).
# Layer 9 is the surface layer; layer 0 is the bottom layer.
speed_layer4 = df["vap_sea_water_speed_layer_4"]

mean_speed = speed_layer4.mean()
p95_speed  = speed_layer4.quantile(0.95)

# Plot the velocity exceedance curve across all sigma layers.
fig, stats = tidal.plot_velocity_exceedance(df)
```

!!! tip "Prerequisites"
    See [Getting Started](../getting-started/index.md) for installation and setup instructions.

### Stage 3: Engineering Design Inputs

Turbine design requires depth-resolved current data. Hub-height selection, blade load estimation, and mooring analysis depend on the vertical shear profile and the distribution of peak speeds through the water column. The full hindcast time series is available via [HSDS](../getting-started/hsds-setup.md) or [AWS S3](../getting-started/aws-s3.md) and supports:

- **Hub-height selection** - identifying the sigma layer with the highest time-mean speed and acceptable shear
- **Blade load analysis** - quantifying the speed differential across the rotor swept area
- **Mooring and foundation design** - characterizing peak spring-tide forces
- **Annual energy production modeling** - applying a turbine power curve to the full speed time series

